#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253605 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172041 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "75710e99-e13c-54ba-9401-486bfec15284" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Rectangular Regions Where a Slope Field Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02b2c05c-45aa-52f2-926a-dfe4e47c4cb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250577} {:answer/id #uuid "088934bb-2f89-5d2f-8bf7-d3aa09fc9883" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250626} {:answer/id #uuid "aa0234ac-b1e7-5745-8e71-c415216e56b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252023} {:answer/id #uuid "aca7731a-ce6a-5b20-93d0-7c22b7484036" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252058} {:answer/id #uuid "c0a81237-6f56-5be3-abce-b5757d269f65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252254}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0a81237-6f56-5be3-abce-b5757d269f65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252254} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6178889-d0c3-5deb-a263-3cfc60399060" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253205}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dff46479-0340-5239-b8ff-db620f2c9a7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-119800" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(1-x)(y-2)$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point $(x,y)$ where $x<1$ and $y>2,$ the slope of the slope field is negative\n- At every point $(x,y)$ where $x>1$ and $y<2,$ the slope of the slope field is negative\n- At every point $(x,y)$ where $x<1$ and $y<2,$ the slope of the slope field is positive" :question/worked-solution "First, we define $f(x,y)=(1-x)(y-2).$ Then, our differential equation is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y)=0$ when $x=1$ or $y=2.$ This divides the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](images/47/47784a3201e93648e495827b12078073cc9f1a03cd3c11904a875bad10a3bde2.png)\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each of those four regions (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/73/73631c178777b4489e35fa87d08474ccf2b8b484b9bf5fe7272b1ce25d45f1b0.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. If $x<1$ and $y>2,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement II is false. If $x>1$ and $y<2,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement III is false. If $x<1$ and $y<2,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in this region.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253607 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172041 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "75710e99-e13c-54ba-9401-486bfec15284" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Rectangular Regions Where a Slope Field Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2869e252-5aad-5585-a2f8-08a41606bfde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250899} {:answer/id #uuid "54614a4d-c109-5408-909c-8c54b4b4c5c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251243} {:answer/id #uuid "b77ba196-db26-538f-8e5a-e02891425df4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252170} {:answer/id #uuid "cf547f70-e98c-5aed-bbd4-8b339b622658" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252383} {:answer/id #uuid "ff12155d-ea78-55f5-90a2-6de9c9364d1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252794}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54614a4d-c109-5408-909c-8c54b4b4c5c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251243} :answer-field/id #uuid "847c24e9-f310-5636-8ed5-8312caef5fb7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253027}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2a6ca6d7-d1ef-5811-9457-4d7cf9a54890" :question/math-academy-id "q-119816" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(x-2)^{2}(y+1)$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point $(x,y)$ where $x<2$ and $y>-1,$ the slope of the slope field is negative\n- At every point $(x,y)$ where $x<2$ and $y<-1,$ the slope of the slope field is negative\n- At every point $(x,y)$ where $x>2$ and $y>-1,$ the slope of the slope field is positive" :question/worked-solution "First, we define $f(x,y)=(x-2)^{2}(y+1).$ Then, our differential equation is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y)=0$ when $x=2$ or $y=-1.$ This divides the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](images/ca/caa61190f1a5565c58838556529715c08f9f00402c6f4b3ccb99a914e748f198.png)\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each of those four regions (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/d8/d836463631120853e05b3b5fb9e534822cf5611747a3eb17e22333901bfb8fd3.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. If $x<2$ and $y>-1,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $x<2$ and $y<-1,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement III is true. If $x>2$ and $y>-1,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in this region.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256685 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172085 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "019e5a17-1aab-565b-8881-447c86a9a7ec" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields on Two-Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7dcdb238-84f5-50d1-81fb-d46208d9425f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254783} {:answer/id #uuid "94b9519b-6e8f-5cb8-8040-62148a141e85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254977} {:answer/id #uuid "a70688af-7165-571e-8ce0-430e79655143" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255143} {:answer/id #uuid "b62432e8-8719-5cba-8d52-19c52dce26b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255281} {:answer/id #uuid "f2f16d7b-50db-57c4-829a-2c1c1353919f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255794}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a70688af-7165-571e-8ce0-430e79655143" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255143} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c523d76-2d1e-5e8c-9b53-1b2e99146620" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255934}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "82c19ae8-c5db-5680-ac18-77dd108b7959" :question/math-academy-id "q-122650" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $U(x,y)=⟨\\frac{1}{x+2},\\sqrt{x-y}⟩$\n- $V(x,y)=⟨x{e}^{y},1-y⟩$\n- $W(x,y,z)=⟨x,-y,-z⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $U(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ne -2,\\,x-y\\ge 0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $V(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ to a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $W(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256687 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172085 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "019e5a17-1aab-565b-8881-447c86a9a7ec" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields on Two-Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c408810-efc6-5638-a618-d28745a9b48a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253767} {:answer/id #uuid "4849dc08-25ab-5a55-b530-c3ded2927c4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254287} {:answer/id #uuid "a6b8d887-618e-5d51-b874-9c8b99a43860" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255139} {:answer/id #uuid "a90bb982-3a8e-52d4-919e-59317069be3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255159} {:answer/id #uuid "b7e3eaf3-da05-5aa5-b7ef-f5fd16504087" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255303}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4849dc08-25ab-5a55-b530-c3ded2927c4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254287} :answer-field/id #uuid "321403c5-6d4b-5e0e-88e6-f677e7259264" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256009}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "212db81f-3e39-5c00-88aa-953a8a56ffb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-122653" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $A(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y),\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)⟩$\n- $B(x,y,z)=⟨-2x,3{y}^{3},{z}^{5}⟩$\n- $C(t)=⟨2-{t}^{2},{e}^{t-1}⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $A(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x+y>0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $B(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $C(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$  It assigns points in $R$ (not ${R}^{2}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256693 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172085 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "019e5a17-1aab-565b-8881-447c86a9a7ec" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields on Two-Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49a2f5e2-8b9e-5771-8e4c-130ad318d884" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254303} {:answer/id #uuid "740aab86-ef29-5671-b4b8-e9ccd3a8d22e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254696} {:answer/id #uuid "832c1dab-f56a-57a4-bb5b-347d7aa89125" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254836} {:answer/id #uuid "d057ce44-c181-5fc1-a138-ce9005660990" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255525} {:answer/id #uuid "fd43e487-e4ea-56ad-b5c6-843be58a5b74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255896}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d057ce44-c181-5fc1-a138-ce9005660990" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255525} :answer-field/id #uuid "b10ade94-378e-5853-8e3a-658322f792bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256242}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4f63ae22-12f3-552d-bede-acf1007c6564" :question/math-academy-id "q-122678" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $U(x,y)=⟨(x+2){e}^{y},\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x-y}⟩$\n- $V(x,y,z)=⟨5,{x}^{3}y,1-{y}^{3}⟩$\n- $W(x,y)=⟨x-3{y}^{2},2-y⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $U(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ne y}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $K(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $V(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ to a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256694 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172087 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb34fdb0-198e-507d-a944-9be782b4cfeb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields on Three-Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a0e2775-dae5-5363-a311-a301ca37db9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253890} {:answer/id #uuid "530f8e39-cb60-5d6f-9021-1268081d55d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254396} {:answer/id #uuid "54c2ae82-1e33-50b6-9b45-93678c89f1fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254409} {:answer/id #uuid "63c6f01c-573e-5a49-ba25-157919c201cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254565} {:answer/id #uuid "9062563e-0f92-548e-b519-03e516bdf7b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254938}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54c2ae82-1e33-50b6-9b45-93678c89f1fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254409} :answer-field/id #uuid "28fa9082-3c30-531c-bc27-0dc4d10a64e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255992}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "72c8972f-c700-5c53-9029-c1e143b47247" :question/math-academy-id "q-122685" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $T(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,j+{z}^{2}\\,k$\n- $U(t)=2t\\,i-{t}^{2}\\,j-\\sqrt{t}\\,k$\n- $V(x,y,z)={e}^{-2z}\\sqrt{x+y}$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $T(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point $(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}$ to a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $U(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $V(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{3}$ to functions in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256695 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172087 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb34fdb0-198e-507d-a944-9be782b4cfeb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields on Three-Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39b861f2-69ec-5c65-9508-f7294f45044a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254175} {:answer/id #uuid "72c370f1-172c-5c9f-95ad-985b95828365" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254687} {:answer/id #uuid "770cf01b-45a0-56c4-9961-b8699f72d11f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254724} {:answer/id #uuid "a69d8038-8063-5f8c-8998-13ee7e4f1213" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255138} {:answer/id #uuid "f9a7d368-1751-561c-a4a6-3b4918ec23a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255857}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72c370f1-172c-5c9f-95ad-985b95828365" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254687} :answer-field/id #uuid "ede176c7-0d20-5a1c-a907-6224f17916d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "09fffcb2-a27e-5da4-b160-3d4f79d02cb0" :question/math-academy-id "q-122688" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $D(x,y,z)=5\\,i+{z}^{2}\\,j+(y+x)\\,k$\n- $E(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-\\sqrt{y}\\,j$\n- $G(x,y,z)=x(y+1)\\,i+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y-z)\\,j+{z}^{3}\\,k$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $D(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point $(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}$ to a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $E(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $G(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,y-z\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\\,n\\in Z}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256696 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172087 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb34fdb0-198e-507d-a944-9be782b4cfeb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields on Three-Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ad7b008-31c5-5bbf-abcb-fdeabdee0637" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254050} {:answer/id #uuid "3590623b-9e50-5848-b85f-bcc80ccdb0b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "